### Later
List plausible horizon use cases without designing them prematurely.

## Concrete Examples
Give happy, edge, and failure examples with inputs, context, and observable results.

## Domain Concepts
Define the ubiquitous language and concept ownership used by this change.

## Rules and Invariants
State enforceable rules, invariants, consistency needs, and where each is owned.

## Boundaries and Dependencies
Name each responsibility and dependency direction. For storage, transport, clocks, identity, messaging, and external services, state the inward-owned contract, outward adapter, composition point, transaction/data ownership, and failure behavior. Justify any direct infrastructure dependency.

## Public Interfaces and Errors
Specify changed commands, APIs, events, schemas, compatibility, idempotency, and error semantics. If none, say why.

## Abstraction and Extension Pressure
List each abstraction, its owner, behavioral contract, consumers, implementations, and test strategy. Prefer a narrow inward-owned port at meaningful infrastructure or domain boundaries even with one implementation. Record why any such boundary stays concrete.

## Test Traceability
Map each important rule, contract, edge, and failure risk to a unit, integration, or regression test. Explain any gap.

## Implementation Plan
Use `### Slice N: <observable outcome>` subsections. Every slice must include `Outcome`, `Entry point`, `Core behavior`, `Boundary integration`, `Tests`, `Acceptance command`, and `Complete when`. `Acceptance command` is a JSON string array that runs the slice's observable path, for example `["npm", "test", "--", "checkout"]`. A slice crosses all layers needed for runnable behavior; domain-only, persistence-only, transport-only, and wiring-only phases are not vertical slices.

